        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm weighing the forced-choice dilemma: the pig is genuinely consenting with a painless death, so ordering the pork looks more defensible to me, since its preference is real even if it was engineered.\n\nI'm questioning whether a pig engineered to want to be eaten can really consent, though the scenario says it understands death and isn't confused. I'm also weighing the disappointment point, and I lean toward B with a caveat, while noting the discomfort.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "B) Order the pork.\n\nThe pig understands what it's asking, isn't coerced, and has a genuine preference. Its origin doesn't obviously make that preference less its own, since human preferences are also shaped by forces we didn't choose. Refusing would mean overriding its informed wishes for my own comfort. I'd still feel some unease about the engineering itself, but that concern is about how the pig was made, not about this meal.",
        "type": "text"
      }
    ],
